%% OVERVIEW:
%%   Novelette does not use standard LaTeX graphics commands, such as \includegraphics. You cannot
%% create vector (TiKz) drawings, or include eps/pdf files.
%%
%% A) Allowed Locations: You may never place an image in footnotes or page headers. You may never
%% place an image inline with ordinary text; place it between paragraphs, where the image will be
%% its own paragraph.
%%   Suggestion: Your book's title page should be attractive. Since Novelette does not allow
%% fonts other than its own, you may create a dramatic effect by creating all (or most) of the
%% title page as an image, with rasterized text in decorative fonts.
%%
%% B) File Storage: Image files must be stored in the same folder as the main document, or in
%% a subfolder from there. You may not load files via absolute path, or by using ../ to rise
%% folder level, or by symlinks (soft links). The path may not use the ~ character. No spaces.
%%   This ensures that when you make a backup copy of your main document folder, its images
%% will also be copied.
%%   For testing purposes, Novelette provides a few images in its own tex/lualatex/novelette
%% folder. These images can be used by filename alone, without path.
%%
%% C) File Format: All graphics must be raster png format. They may be monochrome black/white
%% at 1-bit per pixel, or single-channel grayscale (not RGB, no alpha) at 8-bits per pixel.
%%   Recommended resolution (density) is 600 or more pixels/inch (up to 1200) for monochrome,
%% 300 pixels/inch (rarely anything else) for grayscale.
%%   Tech note: The images are not stored in PDF as png format, but are decompressed/recompressed
%% to a png-like format internal to PDF.
%%   In preview and final modes, Novelette inspects image files for PDF/X-1a:2001 conformance.
%% They must meet the above requirements, and have no color profile or private metadata. Although
%% graphics arts experts know how to do this when the image is created, most ordinary users
%% (and some experts) get it wrong. So Novelette provides shell scripts that correct mistakes.
%% See folder 'doc/lualatex/novelette/utilities' for instructions.
%%
%% D) Size: The maximum height of an \image must be specified by [lines=L] option of the \image
%% command, for integer L. If you fail to specify [lines=L], it is an error.
%%   This creates a gap of L normal text lines, in which the \image sits. If shorter than that,
%% the \image is padded. If too tall to fit, error; then the \image is not printed, and a fitting
%% placeholder prints instead.
%%   The maximum width of an \image is the text line width.
%%
%% E) Horizontal Alignment: An image can be aligned to left, right, or center. This is specified
%% by [align=A] option of the \image command. A is one of left, right, center. Default center.
%%
%% F) Vertical Alignment: When an image is shorter than the gap reserved by its [lines=L] option,
%% it will be padded above and/or below. Option [tweak=T] adjusts the padding. [tweak=up] prints
%% the image with all padding below; [tweak=down] prints with all padding above; [tweak==mid]
%% (default) splits the padding. [tweak=drop] is as low as the image can go, lower than down;
%% it should only be used when the image is a scene separator.
%%
%% G) Floats: Pay attention! Novelette floats are similar to standard LaTeX floats, but have
%% slightly different possible choices and behavior.
%%   In some locations, an image may not float; a float request is ignored, and warning issued.
%% No floats allowed in frontmatter, backmatter, or display zones. Elsewhere, which is between
%% paragraphs of ordinary main text in mainmatter, an image may float.
%%   Standard LaTeX allows each image to request one or more float possibilities. Novelette
%% only allows one choice per image, set by [float=F] option of the \image command. F may be only
%% one of: none, here, top, bottom. [float=none] is forced, where floats are disallowed.
%% [float=here] is the default elsewhere, with automatic alternative 'top' if 'here' does not fit.
%%   There are no page floats. Looks ugly in fiction. Instead, create a component (between others)
%% containing the image. If the height of the image exceeds 20 lines, then the component must
%% use the [single] option.
%%
%% H) General Advice: Print services recommend that line art use line width at least 0.75pt.
%% That is useful advice, but it is not enforced by Novelette or by the printers.
%%   Print services, and online user forums, insist that grayscale image avoid very light and
%% very dark grays. Remember that your book will not be printed by a method that is intended
%% for photographic reproduction. When you process images using Novelette's shell scripts,
%% near-black will be forced to black, and near-white will be forced to white.
%%   Novelette expects that your use of images will be rare. If your book is a graphic novel,
%% or other image-intensive work, then Novelette is not for you.
%%
%% I) Placeholders: Novelette wants you to 'write now, do graphics later'.
%%   In draft mode, the \image command does not look for its files. You may use empty braces where
%% the file name should be, or write a brief reminder of what the image (when created) will mean.
%% A placeholder (gray rectangle) will print. The placeholder will have the correct alignment,
%% vertical lines displacement, and float. Only its width is arbitrary. Later, when you have
%% the actual image, it will replace the placeholder at that exact location.
%%   If you already have the image file, \image* (with asterisk) will print it, as long as it
%% fits within its reserved number of lines. Else, error and placeholder. But the image file
%% will not be tested for all conformance specifications, since that takes time.
%%   In preview and final modes, \image and \image* are identical. The image must exist, must fit,
%% and must meet all specifications. Else, error and placeholder.
%%
%% J) Text Color: In short, text cannot be color. It cannot be grayscale. All text must be black.
%%   There is no 'gray ink' in the method that will be used to print your book. If Novelette
%% allowed gray text, the printer would convert it to raster image. Although most printers can
%% do that, online forums suggest that the results are unpredictable, and outside your control.
%%   If you need gray text, rasterize it to a png image, instead of letting the printer do it.
%% Be sure that the text is bold or heavy, and not too small. Test the result on a home printer,
%% using only black ink (no CMY ink).
%%   Tech note: Large-volume commercial presses may have gray ink, even several shades of gray!
%% They are 'spot colors' pre-mixed to specifications, not CMYK. But this is not available to you.
%%   Developer note: In 'dev' mode, Novelette applies gray shades to certain areas, so that
%% layout and alignment can be visualized. This shading is not available to ordinary users.


%% IMAGE: \image[option]{path/to/filename.png}  also starred version.
%%   The optional argument is not truly optional, because it must at least set the number of lines
%% that the image will displace. The default (3 lines) is a placeholder, and is an error.
%%   In draft mode, the  mandatory argument may be left empty, or you may write a very short
%% description (such as "map of Whereville"). This is because \image will not look for a file.
%%   But the starred \image* always looks for a file. In preview and final modes, you must provide
%% the file, whether \image or \image*.
\def\ftype@figure{1} %% Standard.
\def\nvt@imgrulewd{0pt} %% Nonzero in dev mode. Applies to strut that enforces lines=L.
\def\nvt@offspec{{\upshape\normalfont\nvt@aff{RawFeature={+cv99}}\char"FFFD}} %% An error symbol.
\DeclareDocumentCommand\image{sO{}m}{%%
  \begingroup%%
  \ifnvt@infootnote\def\tmpok{0}\else\def\tmpok{1}\fi%%
  \ifnvt@inheader\def\tmpok{0}\fi%%
  \ifnvt@instyle\def\tmpok{0}\fi%%
  \ifnvt@inblock\def\tmpok{0}\fi%%
  \ifnum\tmpok=0\relax%%
    \nvt@err{Cannot place \string\image\space in footnote or page header.^^J%%
      Cannot place \string\image\space in \string\name, \string\subname, %%
      or block environment.}%%
    {\char"FFFD}%%
  \fi%%
  \ifnvt@inscenetext%%
    \nvt@err{Cannot place \string\image\space in \string\scenetext.^^J%%
      Try the \string\sceneimage\space command.}%%
    \def\tmpok{0}%%
  \fi%%
  \ifnum\tmpok=1\relax%%
    \ifnvt@dev\def\nvt@imgrulewd{2pt}\fi%%
    \def\tmpimgalign{0}\def\tmptweak{0}\def\tmplines{0}\def\tmpvalidate{0}%%
    \ifnvt@indisplay\def\tmpfloat{0}\else\def\tmpfloat{1}\fi%%
    \ifnvt@infrontmatter\def\tmpfloat{0}\fi%%
    \ifnvt@inbackmatter\def\tmpfloat{0}\fi%%
    \par\vspace*{-\nvt@bls}\null\setlength\parindent{0pt}%% Begin paragraph.
    \catcode`\~=12\def\zopt{#2}%% Otherwise, ~ would be undetected here.
    \IfBooleanTF{#1}%%
      {\directlua{nvt.parseimage('star','\luaescapestring{\zopt}','\luaescapestring{#3}')}}%%
      {\directlua{nvt.parseimage('','\luaescapestring{\zopt}','\luaescapestring{#3}')}}%%
%%    \ifnum\tmplines>20\relax%%
%%      \ifnvt@compsingle\else\def\tmpimgreturn{0}\fi%%
%%    \fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpimgreturn=0\relax%%
      \nvt@err{Bad \string\image\space option [\zopt].^^J%%
        \spii Format: [lines=L,align=A,float=F,tweak=T]^^J%%
        \spiv L -> Integer 1<=L<=20. Must be set.^^J%%
        \spix Exception: In component with [single] option, max L= lines per page.^^J%%
        \spiv A -> left right center. Default center.^^J%%
        \spiv F -> none here top bottom. Choose only one.^^J%%
        \spix If here, then top is automatic backup.^^J%%
        \spix In frontmatter, backmatter, displays: default none. No other allowed.^^J%%
        \spix Elsewhere: default here.^^J%%
        \spiv T -> down up mid. Default mid.^^J%%
        \spix down ->  Bottom of image at baseline of lowest displaced line.^^J%%
        \spix up -> Top at accented uppercase height of highest displaced line.^^J%%
        \spix mid -> Splits padding above/below image.}%%
    \fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpimgalign=-1\relax\def\imgleft{}\def\imgright{}\fi%% \sceneimage
    \ifnum\tmpimgalign=0\relax\def\imgleft{\hfill}\def\imgright{\hfill}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpimgalign=1\relax\def\imgleft{}\def\imgright{\hfill}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpimgalign=2\relax\def\imgleft{\hfill}\def\imgright{}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmplines=0\relax%%
      \nvt@err{\string\image\space requires [lines=L] option, integer 1<=L<=linesPerPage.}%%
      \def\tmplines{3}%% Placeholder default. Almost always an incorrect value.
    \fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpisfile=0\relax\def\imgfake{1}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpisfile=1\relax%%
      \IfFileExists{#3}{%%
        \def\imgfake{0}%%
      }{%%
        \nvt@err{Missing image file #3.^^J%%
          \spii The path and filename have correct format, but the file is not there.^^J%%
          \spii Either you mis-spelled the path or filename, or no such file.}%%
        \def\imgfake{1}%%
      }%%
    \fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpisfile=2\relax%%
      \nvt@err{Bad image argument {#3}.^^J%%
        \spii There are several possibilities:^^J%%
        \spiv 1. Image filename does not have .png file extension.^^J%%
        \spiv 2. Path to image file is not relative to main document.^^J%%
        \spix Do not use path relative to sub-documents.^^J%%
        \spix Do not use absolute path, or symlink (softlink).^^J%%
        \spiv 3. Image file is not in same folder as main document,^^J%%
        \spix or in subfolder from main document (max 5 levels).^^J%%
        \spiv 4. Path or filename has forbidden characters.^^J%%
        \spix You may use hyphen or underscore, but not space or tilde.}%%
      \def\imgfake{1}%%
    \fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpfloat=0\relax\def\fps@figure{}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpfloat=1\relax\def\fps@figure{!ht}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpfloat=2\relax\def\fps@figure{!t}\fi%%
    \ifnum\tmpfloat=3\relax\def\fps@figure{!b}\fi%%
    \ifnvt@indisplay%%
      \ifnum\tmpfloat>0\relax\nvt@warn{Ignored request for float in no-float location.}\fi%%
      \def\fps@figure{}%%
    \fi%%
    \ifnvt@infrontmatter%%
      \ifnum\tmpfloat>0\relax\nvt@warn{Ignored request for float in no-float location.}\fi%%
      \def\fps@figure{}%%
    \fi%%
    \ifnvt@inbackmatter%%
      \ifnum\tmpfloat>0\relax\nvt@warn{Ignored request for float in no-float location.}\fi%%
      \def\fps@figure{}%%
    \fi%%
    \ifnum\imgfake=0\relax\nvt@realimage{#3}\else\nvt@fakeimage\fi%%
    \nvt@printimage%%
    \par\vspace*{-\nvt@bls}\null%%
  \fi%%
  \endgroup%%
}%%
%%


%% Fake image, placeholder rectangle:
\def\nvt@fakeimage{%%
  \ifnvt@insceneimage%%
    \def\imgwd{2in}\def\imght{.24in}%%
  \else%%
    \edef\imgwd{\fpeval{0.6*\textwidth}pt}%%
    \edef\imght{\fpeval{0.84*\nvt@em + (\tmplines-1)*\nvt@bls}pt}%%
  \fi%%
  \sbox0{{\nvt@gray{8}\rule{\imgwd}{\imght}}}%%
  \def\imglabel{{\nvt@gray{4}\nvt@aff{RawFeature={+titl},Scale=.75}\tmplabel}}%%
}%%
